lly only  reached the tip of the iceberg in terms  of delivering genetic information to  them with a fifth of them receiving  carrier screening tests and a third of  them receiving non-invasive prenatal  testing the market for inherited cancer  screening is even larger with more  opportunities for growth with 19 million  women who meet criteria for inherited  cancer screening and only 1.5 of them  actually receiving preventative testing  so how do we do this we're a clinical  lab and how do we actually test these  patients for these different diseases so  it turns out like the typical flow of a  sample is actually quite similar across  all of our products we essentially  receive a tube of blood or saliva from  the patient and then we sequence the  sample in our lab depending on those  product we sequence we will do either  high coverage targeted sequencing or low  coverage whole genome sequencing after  sequencing is complete we align the  reads to the reference genome and look  for variance in that samples genome  after going through QC it then moves  into the variant interpretation phase  variant interpretation is the step that  is to determine which variants are  disease-causing or not not all variants  are actually disease-causing and so we  need to find the ones that are and  actually calculate risk as well in this  step so finally once we have all of the  interpreted information we are ready to  send the report to the patient so in  this talk I'm going to focus on how we  use GC p2 for the problem of variant  interpretation so just let's so first  let's talk about some of the challenges  of variant interpretation the challenges  of rendering variant interpretation are  come in two different flavors  one is the scalability flavor and then  the other is the diverse set of  information that we need to interpret  variants so to really understand the  challenge of variant interpretation we  first need to understand the scale of  the problem so how much data are we  dealing with  so myriad as myriad we are the largest  molecular diagnostics  company and we have tested over two  million patients to date all of our  tests are focused which means that we  test specific genes and diseases that we  have predetermined are actionable each  product we test a different set of genes  and diseases but in total and we test  for over 200 genes and diseases so what  does this mean in terms of base pair  sequence it again this depends on our  product but we can think of on average  sequencing around 1 million base pairs  per person some of our tests do low  coverage whole genome sequencing and  some of our other tests have a smaller  region of interest but on average we  will sequence around 1 million base  pairs per person so on the database  level what does this mean we need to  keep track of all of the sequence the  region sequenced in a person so to kind  of like blow that into perspective we  want to keep track of the 1 million  times 2 million base pairs that we've  ever sequenced in the lab we also want  to keep track of the variants that we  see per person so most of the time the  genomic sequence of a person is the same  as the average person variability and  humans are estimated to be 1 in 1,000  base pairs so a naive database  representation of the number of variants  that we see per person would be a  million divided by a thousand which is  1,000 variants per person multiplied by  the two million patients that we have  tested or two billion rows so so that's  kind of that gives you an idea of the  amount of sequencing data that we need  to keep track of for variant  interpretation we are we want to know  what the number of novel variants are  because we want to determine for each of  area and whether or not it's  disease-causing or not  so this graph just shows that even  though you've been testing for a while  there is a long tail of rare variants  that exist a vast majority of these  variants are benign but a small  percentage 