umber of times a certain  genotype is seen in a population so  since humans are diploid we have two  copies of each of our chromosome one is  from our mom then one is reminder so  most people are the same as the  reference genome and so that makes them  homozygous for the reference sequence  some people have a mutation on one copy  of their chromosome making them  heterozygous and sometimes a person has  a mutation on both copies of their  chromosome making them homozygous for  the alternate sequence  and in some rare cases a person actually  only has one copy of the chromosome at a  particular locus making them what we  call a hemizygous  so for variant interpretation we're  really interested in knowing the  zygosity count because if several people  are homozygous for the alternate  sequence then that is that makes this  allele less likely to be disease-causing  all of these questions really fall under  a class of analyses called tertiary  analysis where we try to make sense of  sequencing data so just to contrast  between primary analysis which is really  trying to interpret the data that's  coming off of the sequencing machines  and output sequencing reads and  contrasted with secondary analysis which  aims to assemble and align the reads to  a genome and call variants tertiary  analysis tries to make sense of this  variation in a biological way so other  types of questions that we may want to  ask when we're doing tertiary analysis  would be how often do we see a  combination of alleles together in one  patient we also might want to calculate  these metrics on different segments of  the population so we may want to segment  by ethnicity for example sometimes an  allele can have a high frequency and one  ethnicity but not others and that would  give it enough evidence to classify it  as benign we may also want a segment by  gender because there are some diseases  like cystic fibrosis that have different  outcomes for gender and so you might  want to segment look 