e and do our own  retrospective analyses so again when  we're doing research we have to be very  careful making sure that our samples  have been consented for research and our  research in research allow jurisdictions  but once we have that population then we  can for example ask questions like what  is our carrier rate to understand more  of the efficacy of expanded carrier  screening especially in a diverse like a  diverse ethnic population so what is the  carrier rate what is the address couple  rate those are all very interesting  questions to basically prove efficacy of  our tests with this platform we can also  observe other patterns such as coupling  patterns across ethnicities  and we can observe many other types of  these analyses with the data that we  have so hopefully this so really  hopefully like this motivates building a  system for tertiary analysis that  requires tackling two major challenges  the first one is scalability and the  second is flexibility  we need a flexible system because unlike  clinical trials or other hypothesis  different projects the set of mated  metadata that might be relevant for  analysis is diverse and potentially  unknown upfront so for example I can ask  to see all of the homozygous individuals  that have a co-occurring pathogenic  mutation a one group may want to see a  certain set of metadata for variant  interpretation and another group may  want to see another set of metadata for  retrospective analysis furthermore  different groups may have different  restrictions and access to data so we  really need a solution that allows for  flexible ad hoc aggregation and cyber  filtering of variant information along  with sample metadata we also need a  scalable system we have a very large  population data set and this population  data set is growing every single day so  unlike some of the public databases or  academic cohorts this is a patient  population that is growing every single  day and we need to calculate these  metrics on the latest information so I  just wanted to talk about one  implementation that we had before using  bigquery so we essentially hand-built a  batch compute with Luigi to calculate  allele frequencies this pipeline was  very optimized for the one metric that  it needed to calculate we basically took  advantage of the fact that this was a  recursive procedure and stored the  previous days a CA and AF and would add  to that as needed as we got new samples  every single day  but some of the problems and that we ran  into was that it was not flexible so if  we wanted to add another metric we had  to build a completely different pipeline  and these would often take weeks to run  on all of our historical data we could  obviously like increase the instance  size and the compute power but it would  still be very compute-intensive  the other thing is that it's really not  able to do like ad hoc aggregation and  filtering you really have to be thinking  about what is the information that you  want upfront and then and then building  the pipeline to calculate it so it's  very it's not a very flexible system  it's not scalable in the sense that we  aren't able to really generate these  metrics in an ad hoc way with the amount  of data that we have in the time that  scientists need to see them in real time  so that's kind of motivates why we  decided to look into using bigquery so  this is just a very high-level  architectural overview of our system we  have genomic data and sample data that  is stored on the on Google Cloud storage  this is based this is ingested into  bigquery using data flow and then right  now we have just a very simple system  that persists the results back into  cloud storage so as new data comes in  new we add new rows to bigquery so let  me just go into each of the individual  steps so this is the input data that  we're dealing with we store genomic data  and sample data I will go into a little  bit more detail about what the genomic  data looks like 