 split this up  into two steps really the first is to  parse the genotype field so that every  single row represents a single allele  for a sample  and then after we do this after we  flatten at the table we are able to  aggregate all the alleles together to  generate the LDL count and so this is  kind of a so-so this is like a simple  group buy and then and then account and  aggregate so I wanted to talk a little  bit about some of the tricky cases that  we have dealt with because on the  surface level this may seem like very  straightforward to do but there are some  tricky edge cases that we have to deal  with and one of them is the  deduplication of samples so there's a  possibility that patients can get tested  multiple times in our lab and so as a  result there can be multiple samples  that are associated with a particular  patient and we want to use the most  recent sample because those are assumed  to be the most accurate in our previous  pipeline in fermentation we had to do  this by calculating them at since we  were calculating the metrics  incrementally we had to basically  subtract out the counts of the samples  that were duplicate and and and append  that to append the subtraction to our  final allele count but with bigquery you  can kind of do it it's like much easier  to duplicate the samples upfront and  then kind of recompute your metrics so  the other tricky case that I wanted to  talk about is the deduplication of  alleles and bigquery we often define  there can be multiple representations of  the same allele so when working with  genomic data we often define alleles in  according to the reference sequence but  as you guys may know there are many  versions of the reference genome and  even with the same reference genome  there are potentially multiple  representations in very repetitive  regions so this particular example is an  example of an insertion deletion and in  Dell in a repetitive region of the  genome and you can see that there are  potentially four different  representations of this allele when  compared to the reference genome so this  is a known problem in the field of  genomics there are many groups out many  working groups out there one  and the Global Alliance for genomic  health that are dedicated to solving  this problem so how we've decided to  address this problem is very similar to  the way that Global Alliance for genomic  health has also addressed it by using a  content-addressable hash so this is an  internally built library that we built  that can is used to tag alleles up front  so then we'll tag them with the hash and  then come and then it makes it much  easier to combine ovules later and the  bigquery pipeline and then so finally  the the real advantage of bigquery is  this continuous ingestion of data we're  able to essentially just add rows to the  bakery table as we see more samples  coming through our lab and because it's  so easy to query on this we can kind of  we can just recompute our metrics on the  entire table so how do we so then how do  we actually add more genomic  calculations in bigquery it's as simple  as writing another sequel query so we  can do this and do this for  co-occurrence zygosity and dependent  samples furthermore this putting it in  bigquery allows us to do a lot of ad hoc  filtering and segmentation we can filter  and segment on ethnicity and gender  testing indication etc and so this  really allows us the scalability that we  were looking for and the flexibility  that we were looking for in terms of ad  hoc filtering so to kind of wrap up I  wanted to talk about application in  cystic fibrosis cystic fibrosis is one  of the most common autosomal recessive  conditions according to ACOG it's caused  by a mutation in the CFTR gene which  codes for the CFTR protein CFTR protein  acts as a channel on the the membranes  of the cells to control for the flow of  water and ions in and out of the cell  especially in the lung so if there's the  pathogenic mutation  on both chromosomes this will cause  cystic fibrosis it does this by  basically reducing the flow of these  ions and and as a result there's a thick  mucus that tends to form on the surface  at the cells so this causes lung  infections and limits the ability to  breathe over time so as a carrier  screening lab we have the advantage of  having a very large healthy population  so with this very large healthy  population we're actually able to use  our internal data to reclassify some  variants that we previously did not have  enough evidence to classify and as a  result they were considered variants of  unknown significance variants of unknown  significance are just variants that  we've exhausted all the information we  know about this but we still don't know  whether or not it's disease-causing or  not these types of variants makes it  really difficult to counsel patients  because it's obviously better to know  definitively what are the whether it's  benign or pathogenic so this particular  variant we wanted to know how many  patients have this allele along with  another pathogenic variant if this  particular allele occurs very often with  another pathogenic variant it's much  less likely that this is actually a  pathogenic variant because the fact that  we have a healthy population means that  these people are alive and well and they  they don't have cystic fibrosis so we  asked that question and it turns out  that there were quite a few people that  had a co-occurring mutation with a  pathogenic mutation but then we wanted  to go a little bit further so cystic  fibrosis is an interesting disease and  that there is variable phenotype for  males and so it's very likely that there  may be males with cystic fibrosis I  don't even know it and so to filter out  the males and just look at the number of  co-occurring pathogenic mutations in a  female population that gives us a much  stronger statistical power to classify  this variant as been  and ultimately we were able to do that  we were able to downgrade this variant  to benign this is another example where  we actually did not have enough  information to downgrade so we asked the  question how many patients has this  co-occurring mutation has this mutation  co-occurring with a pathogenic mutation  and the conclusion was that there was  not enough to downgrade and then finally  for this particular variant we also were  able to reclassify it but we actually  reclassified it from pathogenic to the  u.s. given the answers to some of these  questions we actually think it's  probably benign but the fact that we're  not giving a false positive to a patient  is actually very like is is better than  that so to downgrade this Ferenc we  actually use both allele frequency and  co-occurring statistics to to downgrade  so it turns out that this allele  actually occurs quite a lot in a healthy  patient population so it's considered to  be very like a very strong candidate for  actually being benign  [Music]

